CH = dict(num=11, title="Patterns in Life: Diversity and Classification", tag="Biology", theme="Biodiversity, five kingdoms, plant and animal groups, binomial naming",
 intro="Earth holds an immense variety of life. To study it, scientists group organisms by shared features and evolutionary links. This chapter covers biodiversity, how classification evolved, the Five Kingdom system, the groups of plants and animals, the hierarchy of ranks, binomial naming, fossils and threats to biodiversity.",
 big_questions=["What is biodiversity and why does it matter?", "On what basis are plants and animals classified?", "How does classification help in farming and conservation?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "11.1 Biodiversity and India as a Hotspot"),
 ("ul", ["**Biodiversity** is the variety of life forms in all habitats. It keeps nature stable: ocean algae release much of our oxygen, fungi and bacteria decompose waste and make soil fertile, birds, bees and bats pollinate, plants capture sunlight to feed nearly all life. Humans depend on it for food, shelter, medicine and livelihood.", "Farmers conserved crop varieties with traits such as drought tolerance, pest resistance and growth in poor soil; **diversity reduces the risk of crop failure** and strengthens food security.", "**Endemic species** are found naturally only in one region: Nilgiri tahr, Lion-tailed macaque, *Nepenthes khasiana* (Indian pitcher plant), Neelakurinji.", "**Biodiversity hotspot**: a region with many endemic species that has suffered major habitat loss. Hotspots touching India: **Western Ghats, Indo-Burma (incl. North-East India), Himalayas, Sundaland (incl. Nicobar Islands)**.", "India's varied landscapes (mountains, desert, rainforest, plateau, coasts) support many species. Ancient Indian ideas such as the Sangam *Tinai* landscape classification and sacred groves protected local habitats."]),
 ("p", "Present biodiversity is the outcome of small differences among individuals that helped some survive and reproduce, accumulated over many generations."),
 ("h2", "11.2 Why and How to Classify"),
 ("p", "The same organism can fit different groups depending on the **criterion** used (for example by eating habit: carnivore, herbivore; or by activity: diurnal, nocturnal). Scientists therefore choose features that reflect real relationships. Using the library analogy, books must be arranged to be found."),
 ("table", ["Criterion", "What is looked at"], [
   ["External features", "Shape, size, body organisation"], ["Mode of nutrition", "Autotrophic or heterotrophic"],
   ["Internal structure", "Skeleton, organs, tissues"], ["Cell structure", "Unicellular/multicellular, prokaryote/eukaryote, cell wall present or absent"],
   ["Ecological role", "Producer, consumer, decomposer"], ["Reproduction", "Asexual and/or sexual"], ["Genetic similarity", "DNA similarities"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "Similar features suggest **common ancestry**. **Biological classification** is the scientific system of grouping organisms by similarities and differences."),
 ("h3", "Uses of classification"),
 ("ul", ["Makes study organised and systematic.", "Shows similarities, differences and relationships among organisms.", "Helps identify and name new organisms; gives scientists worldwide a common language.", "Supports conservation by identifying threatened organisms.", "Case study — **Pakke Tiger Reserve** (Arunachal Pradesh): about 300 bird species (India has about 1,300) and four hornbills (Rufous-necked, Oriental Pied, Great, Wreathed) which need large old cavity trees and specific fruits, so classifying helps study distribution and links; losing old trees would endanger them."]),
 ("h2", "11.3 Classification Systems Over Time"),
 ("table", ["Scientist / year", "System", "Groups"], [
   ["Aristotle (4th c. BCE)", "Artificial; by habitat/appearance", "Land, water, air animals (limited: only visible features). Rigveda and Brihat Samhita also grouped animals by habitat"],
   ["Linnaeus (18th c.)", "Two kingdom", "Plantae (fixed, make own food) and Animalia (move, depend on others). Problem: *Amoeba*, *Paramecium*, bacteria"],
   ["Haeckel (1866)", "Three kingdom", "Added **Protista** for unicellular microscopic life"],
   ["Copeland (1938)", "Four kingdom", "Monera, Protista, Plantae, Animalia (bacteria lack a true nucleus)"],
   ["Whittaker (1969)", "**Five kingdom**", "Monera, Protista, **Fungi**, Plantae, Animalia (fungi are heterotrophic absorbers, not plants)"],
   ["Carl Woese (1977)", "Three domain (genetic data)", "Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya"]]),
 ("p", "Classification keeps changing as tools (microscopes, staining, DNA studies) improve: science is an ongoing process of reasoning."),
 ("h2", "11.4 The Five Kingdom Classification"),
 ("p", "Basis: **cell type** (prokaryote or eukaryote), **cell structure** (cell wall present or not), **level of organisation** (unicellular or multicellular) and **mode of nutrition** (autotrophic or heterotrophic)."),
 ("table", ["Kingdom", "Cell type", "Body", "Cell wall", "Nutrition", "Examples"], [
   ["**Monera**", "Prokaryote", "Unicellular", "Present", "Auto- or heterotrophic", "Bacteria, archaea, cyanobacteria"],
   ["**Protista**", "Eukaryote", "Unicellular", "Absent or cellulose", "Auto- or heterotrophic", "*Amoeba*, *Paramecium*, *Euglena*, *Chlamydomonas*"],
   ["**Fungi**", "Eukaryote", "Mostly multicellular (yeast unicellular)", "**Chitin**", "Heterotrophic (absorb)", "Mushroom, yeast, *Aspergillus*, *Penicillium*, bread mould"],
   ["**Plantae**", "Eukaryote", "Multicellular", "**Cellulose**", "Autotrophic (photosynthesis)", "Mosses, ferns, pines, roses"],
   ["**Animalia**", "Eukaryote", "Multicellular", "Absent", "Heterotrophic", "Ant, frog, bird, human"]]),
 ("h3", "Monera"),
 ("ul", ["Found everywhere: soil, water, air, hot springs, inside bodies, in the gut of ruminants (source of biogas from dung).", "Harmful ones are pathogens; useful ones include *Lactobacillus* and *Rhizobium*; some break down oil, pesticides and sewage.", "**Cyanobacteria** (blue-green algae) are autotrophs and were among the first to release oxygen (~2.5 billion years ago); fossils form **stromatolites** (found in Rajasthan and Madhya Pradesh).", "Ram Bux Singh, 'Father of Modern Biogas', set up India's first scientific biogas plant in 1957 at Ramnagar, Sitapur."]),
 ("h3", "Protista"),
 ("p", "Microscopic single-celled eukaryotes of water and moist places (found in a **hay infusion**: amoeba, paramecium, euglena). Some are autotrophs, others heterotrophs; they are links in aquatic food chains, produce oxygen, feed small animals or act as decomposers."),
 ("h3", "Fungi"),
 ("ul", ["Cell wall of **chitin**; absorb food from dead and decaying matter through fine filaments forming a **mycelium**; mostly **saprophytes** (decomposers recycling nutrients, keeping soil fertile). Some are mutualistic partners, some are parasites.", "Reproduce sexually and asexually, often by **spores**; best in warm, moist conditions.", "Uses: *Aspergillus* and *Penicillium* give enzymes and antibiotics; mushroom farming needs little space and takes 30–45 days. Folk taxonomy helps tribal communities tell edible from poisonous mushrooms."]),
 ("h3", "Plantae — five classes"),
 ("table", ["Class", "Body", "Water needed?", "Seeds / flowers", "Examples"], [
   ["**Thallophyta** (algae)", "Undifferentiated **thallus**; mostly aquatic", "Live in water", "None", "*Spirogyra*"],
   ["**Bryophyta**", "Slightly differentiated; **rhizoids**, no vascular tissue", "Needs water for reproduction; 'amphibians of the plant kingdom'", "None", "Moss, *Marchantia*"],
   ["**Pteridophyta**", "True roots, stem, leaves; **xylem and phloem**", "Needs water for reproduction", "No seeds", "Ferns"],
   ["**Gymnosperm**", "Needle/scale leaves reduce water loss; cold, dry regions", "Not needed for fertilisation", "**Naked seeds** on cones, no fruit", "Pine, cycads"],
   ["**Angiosperm**", "Most complex; well-developed roots, stem, leaves", "Not needed", "**Flowers, fruits** enclosing seeds; most diverse", "Gulmohar, rose, mango"]]),
 ("ul", ["Flowers attract pollinators; fruits aid seed dispersal (animals, wind, water). Angiosperms can be grouped into monocots and dicots by leaf venation and other features.", "**Lichens** (e.g. *patthar ke phool*) are a symbiosis of an alga (makes food) and a fungus (protects); they are **bioindicators of air quality** and are used for dyes, spice and medicine; some are poisonous.", "Trend: plants evolved from water dependence to transport tissue, seeds and flowers, adapting to land.", "*Hortus Malabaricus* (17th c., Hendrik van Rheede with Itty Achudan) recorded hundreds of Indian medicinal plants."]),
 ("h3", "Animalia"),
 ("p", "Multicellular, heterotrophic, with locomotion and quick response to stimuli. The main criterion is the **notochord** (a flexible rod-shaped structure that can become the vertebral column): animals are **non-chordata (invertebrates)** or **chordata** (protochordates and vertebrates)."),
 ("table", ["Phylum", "Key features", "Examples"], [
   ["**Porifera** (pore-bearers)", "Multicellular, **no tissues/organs**; pores let water carry food and oxygen; fixed, aquatic", "Sponges (1 kg can filter up to 24,000 L water a day)"],
   ["**Cnidaria**", "True **tissues**; tentacles capture prey; one opening for food and waste", "*Hydra*, jellyfish, corals"],
   ["**Platyhelminthes**", "**Bilateral symmetry**, flat body, one opening; many parasitic with hooks and suckers", "Flatworms (tapeworm)"],
   ["**Nematoda**", "Cylindrical; **two openings** (mouth and anus); organ system level; separate sexes", "Roundworms"],
   ["**Annelida**", "**Segmented** body, body cavity, muscles and nerve cord; organ systems", "Earthworm"],
   ["**Arthropoda**", "Jointed appendages, segmented body, hard **external skeleton** (protection, less water loss)", "Insects, crabs, spiders"],
   ["**Mollusca**", "Soft body, often a protective shell; head, muscular foot, hump", "Snail, squid, octopus"],
   ["**Echinodermata**", "Spiny skin; hard **internal skeleton** of calcium carbonate; no notochord", "Starfish, sea urchin"]]),
 ("ul", ["Trend: simple → tissues → bilateral symmetry → two openings → segmentation → exoskeleton / endoskeleton: more complexity but new challenges.", "**Protochordates** (*Amphioxus*) have a notochord at some life stage — a transition from invertebrates to vertebrates.", "**Vertebrates** have a backbone protecting brain and spinal cord and permitting larger size; five groups: **fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals**.", "Adaptations: fins and gills (fish), feathers and hollow bones (birds), fat storage (camel), thick fur (polar bear), mammary glands (mammals)."]),
 ("p", "Parasitic worms enter through contaminated water and food, so wash hands, cook food and drink boiled or filtered water. Diverse forests also protect (mangroves reduced damage in the 1999 Odisha super cyclone; diversity lowers Kyasanur Forest Disease risk)."),
 ("h2", "11.5 Hierarchy and Binomial Nomenclature"),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "Hierarchy from broad to specific: **Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species**. Lower down, organisms share more features; each group is part of the one above (like a postal address)."),
 ("table", ["Rank", "Tiger", "Pea"], [["Kingdom", "Animalia", "Plantae"], ["Phylum", "Chordata (sub-phylum Vertebrata)", "Magnoliophyta"], ["Class", "Mammalia", "Magnoliopsida"], ["Order", "Carnivora", "Fabales"], ["Family", "Felidae", "Fabaceae"], ["Genus", "*Panthera*", "*Pisum*"], ["Species", "*P. tigris*", "*P. sativum*"]]),
 ("p", "**Binomial nomenclature** (Linnaeus, 18th century) gives each organism a two-part Latin scientific name, avoiding confusion between local names (tiger = bagh, puli, tigre)."),
 ("ul", ["First word = **genus** (capital letter); second = **species** (small letters). *Panthera tigris* (tiger) and *Panthera leo* (lion) share genus *Panthera*; mango = *Mangifera indica*.", "Written in **italics** when printed, **underlined** when handwritten.", "A **species** is a group of similar individuals that can interbreed and produce offspring."]),
 ("h2", "11.6 Fossils and Threats to Biodiversity"),
 ("ul", ["**Fossils** are preserved remains of organisms in layers of rock, sand and mud; older layers hold simpler forms, newer ones more complex forms — evidence of change over time. Birbal Sahni (BSIP, Lucknow) studied fossil plants.", "Threats: **pollution, deforestation, overuse of resources, climate change**. Losing one species can cause dependent species to decline.", "Purple Frog (*Nasikabatrachus sahyadrensis*), found in 2003 in the Western Ghats, an ancient frog family that lives underground and breeds in monsoon.", "Sangai deer of Loktak's floating **phumdis** (Manipur) was thought extinct in 1951 and rediscovered in 1953; habitat loss now threatens it (IUCN Red List)."]),
 ("h2", "11.7 Quick Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
   ("Define biodiversity, endemic species and biodiversity hotspot.", "Biodiversity is variety of life; endemic species occur only in one region; a hotspot has many endemic species and major habitat loss."),
   ("Name the biodiversity hotspots of India.", "Western Ghats, Indo-Burma (North-East India), Himalayas and Sundaland (Nicobar Islands)."),
   ("Why do we classify organisms?", "For orderly study, to see relationships, name new species, share a common language and plan conservation."),
   ("List the criteria used for five kingdom classification.", "Cell type, cell wall, level of organisation and mode of nutrition."),
   ("Why was the two kingdom system inadequate?", "Organisms like *Amoeba* and bacteria are unicellular and fit neither plants nor animals."),
   ("Why are fungi placed in a separate kingdom?", "They have chitin walls and absorb nutrients (heterotrophic), unlike plants."),
   ("Distinguish Monera and Protista.", "Monera are prokaryotic with no true nucleus; Protista are single-celled eukaryotes with a nucleus."),
   ("Why are bryophytes called amphibians of the plant kingdom?", "They live on land but need water for reproduction."),
   ("How do pteridophytes differ from bryophytes?", "Pteridophytes have true roots, stems, leaves and xylem/phloem; bryophytes have only rhizoids and no vascular tissue."),
   ("Differentiate gymnosperms and angiosperms.", "Gymnosperms have naked seeds on cones; angiosperms have flowers and seeds enclosed in fruits."),
   ("What is a notochord and how does it classify animals?", "A flexible rod-like structure; animals lacking it are non-chordates, those with it are chordates."),
   ("What is the main feature of Porifera, Cnidaria and Platyhelminthes?", "Cellular level (pores), tissue level (tentacles), and bilateral symmetry respectively."),
   ("Write the taxonomic hierarchy.", "Kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species."),
   ("State the rules of binomial naming with an example.", "Genus first (capital), species next (small), italics/underlined: *Mangifera indica*."),
   ("Why are viruses not in the five kingdoms?", "They are acellular, inactive outside a host and lack cellular organisation, so they fit none of the criteria."),
 ])],
 takeaways=["**Biodiversity** keeps ecosystems stable; India has several **hotspots** with **endemic** species.", "Classification groups organisms by cell type, cell wall, organisation and nutrition; **Whittaker (1969)**: **Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia**.", "Plants: **Thallophyta, Bryophyta, Pteridophyta, Gymnosperm, Angiosperm** — progression to land, vascular tissue, seeds, flowers.", "Animals: **notochord** divides non-chordates (8 phyla) from chordates; vertebrates have 5 groups.", "Hierarchy: **Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species**; **binomial name** = Genus + species (Linnaeus).", "**Fossils** show life's history; pollution, deforestation and climate change threaten biodiversity."])
